From: OpenIRD contributors
Subject: [PATCH] mips: div64: spell the high-word divide in C so modern gcc builds it

Same cause as 0001: GCC dropped the MIPS "h" (HI register) asm constraint
after 4.3, so with the pinned gcc 4.9.4 every file that reaches do_div()
with a 64-bit dividend fails with

  include/asm/div64.h:73:3: error: impossible constraint in 'asm'

0001 only had to cover udelay() because the module build never compiled
the built-in objects.  Building vmlinux does (lib/vsprintf.c, kernel/time,
fs/...), so do_div() needs the same treatment.

The asm divided the high word by the base and took the remainder from HI
and the quotient from LO.  Say that in C; the compiler still emits one
divu and reads both registers.  The long division of the low word that
follows (do_div64_32) has no "h" constraint and is left alone, so the
results are bit-for-bit what the vendor kernel computed.

Origin: gpl-derived
---
--- a/include/asm-mips/div64.h
+++ b/include/asm-mips/div64.h
@@ -69,11 +69,16 @@
 	__low = __div; \
 	__upper = __high; \
 	\
-	if (__high) \
-		__asm__("divu	$0, %z2, %z3" \
-			: "=h" (__upper), "=l" (__high) \
-			: "Jr" (__high), "Jr" (__base) \
-			: GCC_REG_ACCUM); \
+	/* \
+	 * Remainder and quotient of the high word -- what "divu" left in \
+	 * HI and LO.  Spelled in C because gcc dropped the MIPS "h" (HI \
+	 * register) constraint after 4.3; the compiler still emits a \
+	 * single divu for the pair. \
+	 */ \
+	if (__high) { \
+		__upper = __high % __base; \
+		__high = __high / __base; \
+	} \
 	\
 	__mod = do_div64_32(__low, __upper, __low, __base); \
 	\
